    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer, so all devices participate in *every* layer's computation.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), and tokens are routed to whichever device owns the expert selected for them.\n\nIn practice, large MoE deployments (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class models, GPT-MoE variants) combine both: attention layers use TP, MoE/FFN layers use EP, and both are overlaid on data parallelism (DP) for request-level scaling. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP inference, their data flow, the collectives they rely on, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block with:\n\n- Attention projections: $W_Q, W_K, W_V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$, output projection $W_O \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$\n- MLP: up/gate projections $W_{up}, W_{gate} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times 4d}$, down projection $W_{down} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4d \\times d}$\n\nWith world size $p$ (TP degree), the standard Megatron-style layout is:\n\n- **Column-parallel** (split output dimension): $W_Q",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
